title ALES77 — Lotus's equal-power auto-pan, ALES11's quarter-circle split swept across the field
prompt recur the itinerary Lindy-first crux-first, take the next agent-doable lap, approve own design rounds, send each finished increment, stop at custody gates
think ALES76's auto-pan closed by naming exactly this next rung — the equal-power auto-pan, ALES11's power.split swept in place of the linear weight, so the panned voice holds constant loudness across the field. Lindy-first the two durable primitives are already proven and untouched: ALES11's equal-power split with its bounded isqrt (the one genuinely new arithmetic the suite ever needed) and ALES76's triangle-swept-position shape. Crux-first the recognition is the twin of ALES76's own: equal-power auto-pan IS ALES11's equal-power pan swept across time — position through the field and position through a triangle period one parameter, only the LAW reading the position changes.
think The genuine difference from ALES76, not a restatement: the linear auto-pan preserved the SUM (left + right == src byte-for-byte) at the cost of a −6 dB centre dip; this rung lets the sum go and preserves the POWER instead — each sample on the quarter circle, w_left² + w_right² ≤ glide_scale² at every sample and within ALES11's proven two-isqrt truncation bound. The field unit doubles as the split's denominator (den = glide_scale = 256, well inside ALES11's fold bound max_pan_den = 4096), so power.split(pos, 256) is reused verbatim — no pan arithmetic re-derived. Each channel is a plain attenuation x·w/glide_scale.
think Because each weight is a non-negative amplitude ≤ glide_scale (isqrt monotone, den² its cap), each channel is a fraction of the dry, both sit in the i16 rail, and the write's saturate is a proven no-op — the tremolo's non-negative kin, NOT the ring modulator's bipolar one (no sign flip, no two's-complement corner). It reads the mono source and writes a fresh StereoClip: the source is READ-ONLY, a stereo render rather than an in-place edit, so it pans the whole clip.
think Faults are exactly ALES76's, because the position window is identical (only the split law downstream differs): BadPeriod (period not a positive multiple of four or past max_clip), BadDepth (negative depth or swing floor past hard-left), BadPan (swing top past hard-right). No BadGain (the denominator is the fixed glide_scale), no BadDelay, no BadRange on a span. Proved the constant-power law under a full swing including sample_min; a hand-computed sweep (128 → 256 → 128 → 0) matches (707 at centre, 1000 at the edge on a steady 1000); the centre lands 707 each side, distinctly louder than ALES76's linear 500 (−3 dB vs −6 dB); a depth-0 auto-pan is a static equal-power pan reaching the field edges exactly; the source is unchanged; both channels stay in the rail; each fault refuses by name.
obs GREEN on the first build across all seven laws — no RED. The constant-power bound wl²+wr² ≤ glide_scale² asserted in the loop and re-proven in the witness within ALES11's truncation bound. tame_style_check bans clean (my new file not in the zero-assert list); the new file carries well above two asserts per fn. Self-approved design round (no custody gate — bounded in-process i16 PCM rendered to a StereoClip, no network/keys/funds/device/speaker/sample-rate). The modulation family's three LFO targets — delay, amplitude, position — now stand under BOTH pan laws, linear and equal-power.
loom witness=ales_auto_pan_power_witness laws=7 green=1 reuses=triangle_delay_at,power.split,StereoClip,glide_scale glide_scale=256 den=256 centre_weight_isqrt=181 constant=power sum_preserved=no saturate=noop snapshot=none source=read_only
